  <data key="d7">{"problem_description": "Design a 1D CNN for SERS bacterial classification (3 classes: E.coli, Enterococcus, Citrobacter). Raw data is spectral time-series. Historical experiments show: 1) Depth correlates with accuracy but causes overfitting (8.9-23.5% train-test gaps), 2) High dropout (~0.68) + high weight decay (~0.0023) work synergistically, 3) Need to control capacity and avoid parameter inflation, 4) Consider residual connections, batch normalization, and Global Average Pooling instead of FC layers.", "constraints": "Model must be 1D CNN compatible with time-series data. Target input shape from SERS datasets is (sequence_length, 1). Should be deep (8-12 layers) but with controlled parameter count. Must include regularization techniques to address overfitting. Use residual connections for better gradient flow in deep networks.", "inspiration": "Historical SERS experiments showing depth-accuracy correlation, ResNet-style skip connections for deep networks, synergistic regularization regime (high dropout + high weight decay), and need for Global Average Pooling to reduce parameters.", "depth": 0, "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "unknown_manifest"}</data>
  <data key="d8">[0.023786500000000002, 0.09362031549811364, 0.0008044177808544867, 1.874202733039856, 0.8320405311584473]</data>
</edge>
