<edge source="1D_CNN_d8ee7d55" target="1D_CNN_d8ee7d55" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 25 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 25 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 25 (for the last block of research):\n **Strategic Lesson: From Capacity Accumulation to Capacity-Regularization Balance**\n\n### What to Keep\n- **The Conv-Dense Asymmetry**: The evidence that fewer dense units (70 vs. 102) combined with more convolutional layers (5 vs. 3) drives performance is robust. Preserve this \"wide conv, narrow dense\" architectural template.\n- **Conservative Optimization**: The lower learning rate (0.0004) and higher dropout (0.57) observed in top trials indicate that stability and regularization are critical; do not revert to high-LR, low-dropout regimes.\n- **Convergence Stability as a Leading Indicator**: The correlation between stability metrics and final accuracy (when disentangled from circularity) suggests that training dynamics are valid early-stopping criteria for architecture search.\n\n### What to Change\n- **Control for Compute Budget**: The current \"best\" trials confounded architecture with training time (29 vs. 18 epochs). **Fix the epoch budget or measure \"accuracy per FLOP\"** to isolate architectural signal from optimization duration. The depth advantage may vanish if shallow networks are allowed equivalent compute.\n- **Redefine \"Best\" via Composite Score**: Replace single-metric optimization (accuracy) with a **penalized score**: `Accuracy - \u03b1(Overfitting Gap) - \u03b2(Inference Latency)`. An 11% generalization gap and 60% latency increase (13.96ms vs. 8.75ms) make the current \"best\" trials deployment-unready.\n- **Shift from Main Effects to Interaction Analysis**: Stop analyzing hyperparameters in isolation. **Test conditional hypotheses**: Does dropout only help when parameter count exceeds 2M? Does kernel size matter only for specific input resolutions? Does learning rate efficacy depend on batch size (unmeasured)?\n- **Architectural Ablation**: Validate whether 5-layer depth is inherently superior or merely enables longer training via gradient stability. **Test 5-layer networks without skip connections or batch normalization**; if they fail to outperform 3-layer nets, the finding is about trainability, not representational capacity.\n- **Statistical Rigor**: Apply Bonferroni/FDR correction for multiple comparisons; report 95% confidence intervals for correlation coefficients. Treat $r=0.5$ as moderate evidence, not $p&lt;0.001$ as definitive proof.\n\n### New Hypothesis\n**Performance emerges from the interaction of architectural capacity and regularization intensity, not from capacity alone.** Specifically, deep convolutional stacks (\u22655 layers) only outperform shallow ones when paired with aggressive regularization (dropout &gt;0.5) and constrained dense bottlenecks (&lt;80 units), which together enable extended training (more epochs) without overfitting. The optimal configuration lies at the **Pareto frontier of capacity vs. generalization**, where inference cost and overfitting gap are minimized jointly with accuracy.\n\n**Immediate Action**: Run a controlled experiment fixing epochs at 30 for all architectures, and test the interaction term `Dropout Rate \u00d7 Parameter Count` as the primary predictor of the new composite score (accuracy minus overfitting penalty).", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_spectral_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "SERS bacterial classification dataset - E.coli, Enterococcus, Citrobacter. Optimized for spectral peak preservation with moderate frequency resolution."}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.050079136737920006, 0.031383960372985034, 0.004639553955513804, 1.909190020647239, 1.8280901623751213]</data>
</edge>
