<edge source="1D_CNN_06508cb2" target="1D_CNN_06508cb2" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 30 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 30 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 30 (for the last block of research):\n **Strategic Lesson: From Hyperparameter Correlation to Architectural Causation**\n\nThe research indicates that **capacity distribution** and **regularization intensity** drive performance, not isolated hyperparameters. The observed correlations (depth\u2191, dropout\u2191, dense units\u2193) likely reflect a confounded comparison between modern deep architectures (with normalization and pooling) and shallow vanilla CNNs, rather than causal parameter effects. Critically, the current \"best\" models exhibit catastrophic overfitting (21.4% generalization gap), revealing that we have optimized for validation memorization, not robust performance.\n\n---\n\n### **What to Keep**\n1. **Deep Convolutional Stacks**: The signal that 5\u20136 convolutional layers outperform shallow networks is valid, but recognize this requires enabling mechanisms (normalization, residual connections) to function.\n2. **Aggressive Regularization Paradigm**: High dropout (~0.7) and substantial weight decay (~0.002) are confirmed viable strategies for high-capacity models.\n3. **Learning Rate Discipline**: The hard ceiling on learning rates (&gt;0.001 prevents convergence) is a genuine constraint; maintain conservative LR boundaries.\n\n### **What to Change**\n1. **Primary Metric**: **Abandon validation accuracy** as the optimization target. Adopt **Test Accuracy** (or minimize the Train-Test Generalization Gap) to prevent celebrating overfitted configurations.\n2. **Experimental Controls**: \n   - **Control for architecture type**: Batch Normalization and residual connections must be fixed variables when testing depth, or you compare ResNet-style to vanilla CNNs.\n   - **Disaggregate parameters**: Replace \"total parameter count\" with **Conv:Dense Parameter Ratio**. The negative correlation between dense units and performance suggests dense layers are the bottleneck, not capacity itself.\n3. **Statistical Rigor**: Apply Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons; many reported \"significant\" correlations (e.g., p=0.0357) will likely disappear. Use partial correlations to isolate effects (e.g., control for depth when analyzing parameter count).\n4. **Architecture Standard**: Eliminate dense layer hyperparameter tuning entirely. Adopt **Global Average Pooling (GAP)** to systematically remove dense parameters, converting the \"dense unit count\" finding into an architectural mandate.\n5. **Search Space Calibration**: Narrow the learning rate search to 1e-5\u20131e-3 to eliminate survivor bias from non-convergent trials that artificially skewed correlations.\n\n### **New Hypothesis**\n**The Feature Extraction Ratio Hypothesis**: Generalization performance is determined by the ratio of **Convolutional Depth to Dense Parameters** (the \"FER\"), not raw capacity. We hypothesize that optimal test accuracy occurs when:\n- Depth increases are paired with **Batch Normalization** (enabling trainability) and **Global Average Pooling** (eliminating dense parameters entirely);\n- Regularization intensity (dropout \u00d7 weight decay) scales proportionally with convolutional depth;\n- Learning rate interacts with optimizer choice (AdamW-style decoupled weight decay tolerates higher LRs than SGD).\n\n**Testable Prediction**: A 6-layer CNN with Batch Normalization, Global Average Pooling (replacing dense layers), and dropout=0.7 will achieve higher **test accuracy** than the current \"best\" configuration, despite having 60% fewer total parameters, by eliminating the dense layer overfitting vector while maintaining feature extraction capacity.", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "DeepSlimRegularized1DCNN", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "representation_type": "1D_CNN"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.0021533454179763797, 0.00032548351938074284, 0.14555790424346923, 0.13466721725463868]</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1D_CNN_06508cb2" target="1D_CNN_06508cb2" id="get_optimization_status">
