<edge source="1D_CNN_06508cb2" target="1D_CNN_06508cb2" id="memorize_finding(finding=deep_slim_residual_d_cnn.py validation:...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=deep_slim_residual_d_cnn.py validation:...)</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], [-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "deep_slim_residual_d_cnn.py validation: Architecture achieves breakthrough 92.7% peak test accuracy (highest across all architectures) but is critically constrained at 86k parameters. Memory-recommended 600-700k parameter floor is validated - current capacity prevents stable operation (\u03c3 ranges 0.56%-5.76%). Dropout ceiling observed at 0.7 (causes underfitting). Optimal operating region: LR=0.0002, dropout=0.5-0.6, weight_decay=0.002-0.003, batch_size=32. Requesting Innovation Team to propose v2 with 500-700k parameters via increased base filters (48-64) or additional residual blocks while maintaining GAP and residual connections.", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_standard_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "representation_type": "1D_CNN"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.02983739, 0.06290190495640297, 0.01014772696530927, 5.492933686461283, 0.2545210406112671]</data>
</edge>
<edge source="1D_CNN_06508cb2" target="1D_CNN_06508cb2" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 35 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 35 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 35 (for the last block of research):\n**Strategic Lesson: From Scaling Laws to Architectural Efficiency**\n\n### The Core Strategic Pivot\nYour \"best\" models have not discovered an optimal architecture; they have rediscovered **Kaplan scaling laws** (capacity + depth = accuracy). The 11\u00d7 parameter inflation and collinear correlations (depth vs. parameters vs. inference time) indicate you are measuring **model scale**, not architectural design. The next phase must disentangle *capacity* from *structure* and pivot from \"bigger is better\" to \"efficient depth with surgical regularization.\"\n\n---\n\n### What We Retain (Validated Signals)\n1. **Deep Convolutional Stacks**: The depth-accuracy correlation (r=0.851) is robust, but treat this as a **necessary, not sufficient** condition. Depth provides representational power, but independent of width/parameters.\n2. **Aggressive Regularization Chemistry**: The co-occurrence of high dropout (~0.68) and high weight decay (~0.0023) in best trials suggests a **synergistic regularization regime**, not just individual hyperparameter tuning. This combination suppresses the overfitting indicated by the 8.9% train-heldout gap.\n3. **Small (or Zero) Fully-Connected Layers**: The negative correlation with dense units (r=-0.650) is a signal to **eliminate, not just shrink**, FC layers. Transition to Global Average Pooling (GAP) to replace dense classifiers entirely.\n4. **Learning Dynamics as Diagnostics**: Retain \"learning speed\" and \"convergence stability\" not as optimization targets, but as **early-stopping health metrics** for trial viability (addressing survivorship bias).\n\n---\n\n### What We Change (Methodological &amp; Architectural Corrections)\n1. **Control for Capacity**: Run partial correlations (depth vs. accuracy | parameters fixed) or variance decomposition to isolate architectural depth from scaling effects. Test **deep+narrow vs. shallow+wide** at fixed parameter budgets (~1M vs ~4M).\n2. **Expand the Search Space**: The null results for filters and kernel size are anatomically suspicious. Expand ranges (e.g., filters: 16-512; kernel: 1\u00d71 to 7\u00d77) and test if null results persist; if they do, the task may be too simple for spatial hierarchies to matter, warranting architectural simplification.\n3. **Analyze Interactions, Not Main Effects**: Test **Learning Rate \u00d7 Weight Decay** and **Dropout \u00d7 Weight Decay** using ANOVA or random forest feature importance. The failure mode \"high LR + low WD\" suggests an interaction boundary, not independent effects.\n4. **Include the Cemetery**: Incorporate failed trials (NaN losses, crashes) into the dataset to correct for survivorship bias. Calculate conditional failure probabilities (e.g., P(failure | LR &gt; 0.002)) to identify unstable regions of the hyperparameter landscape.\n5. **Pivot to Pareto Optimization**: Abandon \"best vs. worst\" dichotomies. Plot the **accuracy vs. FLOPs/parameters frontier**. The current \"best\" trials are likely **Pareto-dominated** by smaller, equally accurate models you have not yet tested.\n6. **Fix the Generalization Gap**: The 8.9% train-heldout gap indicates memorization, not learning. Introduce data augmentation, label smoothing, or stochastic depth to the deep architectures to test if depth still dominates when overfitting is controlled.\n\n---\n\n### The New Hypothesis\n**\"Architectural efficiency is determined by the depth-to-width ratio and the elimination of fully-connected layers, not raw parameter count.\"**\n\nWe hypothesize that a **deep (&gt;5 conv layers), narrow (&lt;64 filters per layer) network utilizing Global Average Pooling (zero dense units)**, trained with synergistic regularization (high dropout \u00d7 high weight decay) and low learning rates, will achieve equivalent accuracy to current best trials with **&lt;50% of the parameters**, sitting on a superior Pareto frontier. Furthermore, we hypothesize that the null effect of kernel size will reverse when the model is forced to learn hierarchical spatial features (i.e., with reduced capacity/downsampling), revealing that current null results are artifacts of capacity overhang, not architectural irrelevance.\n\n**Next Action**: Execute a constrained-capacity ablation study comparing deep-narrow-GAP architectures against the current wide-shallow-dense baseline at 1M and 4M parameter budgets.", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_standard_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "representation_type": "1D_CNN"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.0393881, 0.03310626576652788, 0.005340908929110142, 2.8910177297164648, 0.13198733963127138]</data>
</edge>
