<edge source="1D_CNN_06508cb2" target="1D_CNN_06508cb2" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 40 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 40 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 40 (for the last block of research):\n **Strategic Lesson: From Correlation to Causal Architecture Design**\n\n### **Core Insight: The Capacity-Regularization Coupling**\nThe observed performance differential is not driven by isolated hyperparameter optima, but by an **emergent capacity-regularization frontier**. Deeper architectures (6+ layers) demonstrate higher accuracy potential, but only when paired with aggressive regularization (dropout ~0.6, weight decay ~0.002). However, the 9.3% train-validation gap in \"best\" trials indicates these models are likely **exploiting validation set noise** rather than achieving true generalization. The learning rate \"correlation\" is artifactual\u2014likely confounded by training budget allocation (lower rates received more effective updates before early stopping).\n\n---\n\n### **What to Keep**\n- **Depth as a capacity multiplier**, but recognize it is currently confounded with total parameters and regularization strength.\n- **Stability metrics** (low `std_accuracy`) as primary selection criteria over single-run peak performance.\n\n### **What to Change**\n1. **Decouple Optimization from Budget**: Fix training by **total optimization steps or convergence criteria**, not epochs/patience, to eliminate the learning rate \u00d7 compute confound. Implement learning rate warmup + cosine annealing to test whether \"low LR\" effects persist when convergence time is controlled.\n\n2. **Move from Marginal to Interaction Analysis**: Stop treating hyperparameters as independent variables. Use factorial designs or Bayesian Optimization to model the **Depth \u00d7 Width \u00d7 Regularization** interaction surface. The current \"best\" settings (deep + high dropout) likely represent a local optimum for over-parameterized models, not a global architectural principle.\n\n3. **Audit the Depth-Width Trade-off**: Test **wide-shallow vs. narrow-deep** architectures with equivalent parameter counts (e.g., 4-layer/640-filter vs. 6-layer/256-filter networks). The null correlation with filter count suggests the search space favored \"tall and skinny\" networks that may suffer from representational bottlenecks.\n\n4. **Validation Hygiene**: The 9.3% overfitting gap and lack of correlation between overfitting score and accuracy suggests **validation leakage or insufficient validation data**. Implement k-fold cross-validation and early stopping on validation loss (not accuracy). If \"best\" models still overfit most aggressively, the 88.1% accuracy metric is unreliable.\n\n5. **Control for Architectural Baselines**: Standardize normalization (BatchNorm/LayerNorm) across all trials. Its absence in the initial report represents a massive uncontrolled variable that likely explains variance attributed to other hyperparameters.\n\n6. **Statistical Rigor**: Apply Bonferroni or FDR correction to previous p-values. Correlations with r &lt; 0.4 (e.g., weight decay r=0.343) explain &lt;15% of variance and are likely spurious in high-stochasticity search spaces.\n\n---\n\n### **New Hypothesis**\n**Optimal performance lies on a regularization-capacity frontier where:**\n- Model depth scales with task complexity, but only when width is sufficient to prevent gradient/feature collapse (depth requires width).\n- Regularization strength scales with model capacity, but current \"high\" values (0.64 dropout) may still be on the left side of the U-curve (under-regularized for the capacity).\n- The \"best\" trials identified so far represent **overfit local optima** that will degrade under rigorous validation or data shift.\n\n**Prediction**: When controlling for compute budget and validation robustness, a **4-layer wide network with moderate regularization** will match or exceed the 6-layer narrow network performance, with superior inference efficiency (18ms \u2192 &lt;15ms) and lower variance across seeds.\n\n---\n\n### **Immediate Action Plan**\n1. **Ablation Study**: Fix 2.5M parameters, compare 4-layer (wide), 6-layer (medium), and 8-layer (narrow) with individually tuned regularization for each depth.\n2. **Learning Rate Robustness**: Re-run top 10 configurations with LR warmup + cosine decay (range 1e-3 to 1e-4) to test if performance is optimization-stable or budget-dependent.\n3. **Overfitting Diagnostic**: Increase validation set size by 50% or implement stratified k-fold. If \"best\" trial accuracy drops significantly, discard current top performers as overfit.\n4. **Regularization Range Extension**: Test dropout up to 0.8 and weight decay up to 0.01 to locate the true regularization ceiling (where performance degrades), confirming the current 0.64 is near-optimal, not just \"higher than worst.\"", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_standard_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "representation_type": "1D_CNN"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.0393881, 0.05422488491472871, 0.006974461751633504, 4.215236476019921, 0.13199078477653714]</data>
</edge>
