<edge source="1D_CNN_ae58392c" target="1D_CNN_ae58392c" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 55 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 55 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 55 (for the last block of research):\n**Strategic Lesson: From Correlation to Capacity-Controlled Causality**\n\n### The Consolidated Insight\nThe observed performance differences are driven primarily by **model capacity** (parameter count), not architectural depth per se. The \"best\" trials succeeded not because they had 6.16 convolutional layers, but because deeper architectures forced a collateral reduction in learning rate and increased regularization, creating a coincidentally stable optimization regime. The 19.5-point train-to-test accuracy gap (0.974 \u2192 0.789) signals **hyperparameter overfitting to the validation set**; the true generalization ceiling is closer to 0.79, not 0.88.\n\n---\n\n### What to Keep\n1. **The Regularization Stack**: The combination of high dropout (&gt;0.6) + weight decay (&gt;0.002) + low learning rate (&lt;0.0003) represents a valid high-capacity training regime. Do not discard these values, but treat them as **jointly necessary** constraints, not independent dials.\n2. **Capacity Scaling**: Larger models (2M+ parameters) do improve accuracy, but only when regularization scales proportionally. Keep pursuing scale, but with stricter regularization budgets.\n3. **Variance Reduction**: The strong negative correlation between convergence stability and accuracy variance (r=-0.430) is mechanistically sound. Prioritize optimization stability metrics as early stopping criteria.\n\n---\n\n### What to Change\n1. **Control for Capacity**: Immediately run **partial correlation analysis** residualizing parameter count. If conv layer count loses significance, pivot from \"depth\" to \"efficient capacity\" (wider vs. deeper trade-offs).\n2. **Eliminate Extreme Groups Analysis**: Abandon \"best vs. worst\" binning. Instead, model the continuous response surface using **median splits with bootstrapped CIs** or Gaussian Process surrogates to avoid tail-selection bias.\n3. **Fix the Validation Protocol**: The 0.879 validation score is optimistically biased. Implement **nested cross-validation**:\n   - Outer loop: Unseen test set (never touched)\n   - Inner loop: Validation for early stopping/hyperparameter selection\n4. **Audit the Dropout Paradox**: High dropout (0.652) + near-perfect training accuracy (0.974) suggests data leakage or incorrect dropout implementation. Verify dropout is active during training and check for augmentation leakage.\n5. **Replace Dense Layers**: Test **Global Average Pooling (GAP)** instead of dense layers. The \"fewer dense units\" finding likely masks over-parameterization in the final layers; GAP will decouple parameter count from spatial hierarchy.\n\n---\n\n### The New Hypothesis\n**\"Optimization Stability Mediates Capacity Utilization\"**\n\nAccuracy is not directly caused by depth or parameter count, but by the **interaction between capacity and optimization stability**. Deep networks (6+ layers) only outperform shallow ones when paired with learning rates low enough to prevent gradient chaos, and regularization high enough to utilize the excess capacity without memorization. \n\nThe previous results conflated three distinct phenomena:\n- **Capacity effect**: More parameters = higher representational power\n- **Optimization effect**: Lower LR + high regularization = better convergence stability  \n- **Depth artifact**: Deeper networks *require* lower LRs to train at all, creating a spurious correlation between depth and accuracy\n\n**Corollary**: For a fixed parameter budget, width may outperform depth if paired with appropriate regularization, but depth forces beneficial optimization constraints.\n\n---\n\n### Next Phase Protocol\n1. **Intervention Study (This Week)**: Fix parameter count at 2.8M. Randomly assign architectures to {Shallow-Wide (3 layers, wide kernels) vs. Deep-Narrow (6+ layers, 3\u00d73 kernels)}. Control LR by depth (deep nets get 0.0002, shallow get 0.001). Test if depth matters when capacity and optimization are decoupled.\n2. **Collinearity Audit**: Report Variance Inflation Factors (VIF) for all architectural variables. If VIF &gt; 5 for depth/parameters/size, merge into a single \"capacity\" dimension.\n3. **Cost-Aware Pareto Frontier**: Plot Accuracy vs. Inference Time (ms) and Accuracy per Million Parameters. Discard any configuration below the Pareto frontier (e.g., if a 1.6MB model achieves 0.85 accuracy vs. 10.81MB achieving 0.879, the larger model may not justify the 6.7\u00d7 size cost).\n4. **Regularization Interaction Test**: Systematically vary dropout (0.3\u20130.7) against weight decay (0.0001\u20130.005) to find the **regularization frontier** where validation loss minimizes before the 0.974 training accuracy threshold (target: keep training accuracy &lt;0.95 to ensure dropout is working).\n\n**Stop doing**: Treating conv layer count as an independent optimization variable.\n**Start doing**: Treating depth as a constraint that dictates the feasible learning rate and regularization budget, with capacity (parameters) as the primary independent variable.", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_spectral_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "SERS bacterial classification dataset - E.coli, Enterococcus, Citrobacter. Optimized for spectral peak preservation with moderate frequency resolution."}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.03915069743210001, 0.03614091031478048, 0.006302883027829999, 3.0398797681580074, 0.07256334988424291]</data>
</edge>
