<edge source="1D_CNN_ae58392c" target="1D_CNN_ae58392c" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 75 (for th...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 75 (for th...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 75 (for the last block of research):\n**Strategic Lesson**\n\n**Keep**  \n- **Low learning rates** (~0.0002\u20130.0005) \u2013 strongly correlated with higher accuracy.  \n- **Moderate to high regularization**: weight decay (~0.002) and dropout (~0.6) \u2013 both associated with better performance.  \n- **Larger architectures**: more convolutional layers (\u22488), more filters (80+), and more dense units (130+) \u2013 these increase capacity and improve accuracy.  \n- **Residual connections** \u2013 present in all best trials, likely enabling stable training of deeper models.  \n\n**Change**  \n- **Combat overfitting**  \n  - Introduce **data augmentation** (e.g., rotations, flips) to increase effective dataset size.  \n  - Add **batch normalization** to convolutional layers for improved stability and additional regularization.  \n  - Replace flatten + dense with **global average pooling** to drastically reduce parameters, then compensate by increasing depth/filters if needed.  \n  - Consider **tighter early stopping** or slightly higher weight decay/dropout to further narrow the train\u2011test gap (currently 9.3%).  \n- **Improve evaluation rigor**  \n  - Define best/worst groups using **validation metrics only**, keeping a separate test set for final reporting.  \n  - Perform **statistical tests** and report confidence intervals to ensure differences are meaningful.  \n  - Clearly define derived metrics (learning speed, convergence stability, overfitting score) for reproducibility.  \n- **Conduct controlled experiments**  \n  - Isolate the impact of residual connections, pooling type, learning rate, and spectral attention through **ablation studies**.  \n  - Explore **hyperparameter interactions** (e.g., depth \u00d7 learning rate) using multivariate analysis or factorial designs.  \n\n**New Hypothesis**  \nA deep convolutional network with residual connections, batch normalization, global average pooling, and strong regularization (data augmentation, weight decay, dropout) will achieve **test accuracy &gt;88%** with a **train\u2011test gap &lt;5%**, while maintaining a **more efficient parameter count** and **better computational trade\u2011offs**.  \nSpectral attention, though currently always used, may be unnecessary; removing it could simplify the model without sacrificing performance if the other enhancements are in place.", "experiment_name": "1D_CNN", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_spectral_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "SERS bacterial classification dataset - E.coli, Enterococcus, Citrobacter. Optimized for spectral peak preservation with moderate frequency resolution."}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.0391435465422768, 0.03614091031478048, 0.008364064751879152, 4.175077153463473, 0.35899582808303837]</data>
</edge>
