<edge source="2D_GAF_0f954a65" target="2D_GAF_0f954a65" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 120 (for t...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 120 (for t...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 120 (for the last block of research):\n### **Strategic Lesson: From Correlation to Causal Architecture**\n\n**What to Keep:**\n- **Strong, reliable signals:** Prioritize tuning **learning rate** (target ~0.0002) and increasing **convolutional depth** (7+ layers) as primary levers for accuracy.\n- **Stability metrics:** Continue tracking **mean learning speed** and **accuracy standard deviation** as early indicators of training health.\n- **The overfitting warning:** The consistent 9%+ train-test gap is a valid red flag; maintain a rigorous held-out test set.\n\n**What to Change:**\n1. **Stop assuming \u201cmore is always better.\u201d** The correlation between complexity and accuracy is weak. Shift from simply adding layers/filters to **designing architectures**.\n2. **Investigate inference cost directly.** The speed-accuracy correlation is moderate. Decouple these by profiling a subset of models to find the **Pareto frontier** of efficient, high-accuracy designs.\n3. **Abandon analyzing hyperparameters in isolation.** The next phase must test **interactions**\u2014especially learning rate \u00d7 optimizer \u00d7 batch size.\n\n**New Hypothesis &amp; Action Plan:**\nWe hypothesize that **structured, efficient architectures with aggressive regularization will outperform simply larger models**, closing the overfitting gap without sacrificing accuracy.\n\n**Next Optimization Phase Priorities:**\n\n1.  **Architectural Experiment:**\n    - **Pattern over Scale:** Test established patterns (e.g., doubling filters per layer, residual blocks) vs. the correlated but unpatterned increases.\n    - **Introduce Explicit Regularization:** Systematically test BatchNorm, Dropout, and stronger L2 decay in combination with low learning rates.\n    - **Fix the Optimizer:** Use Adam or AdamW as a controlled baseline to remove a key variable.\n\n2.  **Diagnostic Analysis:**\n    - **Causal Check:** Run a controlled ablation study. Hold compute constant and vary *only* learning rate or *only* number of layers to isolate effects.\n    - **Find the Failure Threshold:** Identify the **learning rate threshold** (likely near 0.001) where performance collapses.\n    - **Analyze Learning Curves:** Diagnose if the overfitting gap arises late (memorization) or early (insufficient generalization capacity).\n\n3.  **Efficiency Mandate:**\n    - For any new model achieving target accuracy, **benchmark its inference time and parameter count**. The goal is to move the accuracy-efficiency Pareto frontier.\n\n**Bottom Line:** We are done describing. The next phase is **causal, architectural, and efficiency-focused.** Build deeper *by design*, not by correlation, and aggressively manage the overfitting risk that this data has clearly revealed.", "experiment_name": "2D_GAF", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_2D_spectrogram_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 256, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "2D spectrogram for SERS bacterial classification"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
</edge>
