<edge source="2D_GAF_87b7249f" target="2D_GAF_87b7249f" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 135 (for t...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 135 (for t...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 135 (for the last block of research):\n### **Strategic Lesson: The Complexity-Efficiency Frontier**\n\n**Core Insight:** Performance gains from architectural complexity are real but come with severe diminishing returns and overfitting risks. The current approach prioritizes raw capacity over smart design.\n\n---\n\n### **What to Keep \u2705**\n\n1.  **Low Learning Rate + High Weight Decay:** This regularization combo works. Maintain low base learning rates (~0.0002-0.0005) with aggressive weight decay (~0.003-0.005).\n2.  **Architectural Scaling Direction:** Deeper networks (7+ conv layers) with moderate width (~75 filters) outperform shallow ones. Continue exploring this region.\n3.  **Training for Convergence:** Longer training (40+ epochs) with higher learning speed metrics correlates with stability. Maintain sufficient training budget.\n\n### **What to Change \ud83d\udd04**\n\n1.  **Eliminate the Batch Norm Confound:** The -1.0 correlation is a design artifact. Run a controlled ablation: identical architectures with/without batch norm, matched for training epochs and LR.\n2.  **Shift from \"Larger is Better\" to \"Smarter is Better\":**\n    *   **Test Modern Components:** Immediately trial residual connections and swish/leaky ReLU activations alongside current designs.\n    *   **Implement Aggressive Regularization:** For deep models (\u22657 layers), increase dropout (0.3-0.5) and add spatial dropout (0.1-0.2) to address the 8.8% generalization gap.\n    *   **Control for Capacity:** Fix parameter budget (~1M params) and test depth vs. width trade-offs systematically.\n3.  **Fix Training Protocol Gaps:**\n    *   Implement cosine annealing LR schedules starting from your proven low base rates.\n    *   Standardize batch size (start with 64) across trials to remove this hidden variable.\n    *   Add early stopping based on validation loss divergence, not just epoch count.\n\n### **New Hypothesis to Test \ud83e\uddea**\n\n**\"Optimal performance requires balancing depth with explicit anti-overfitting design, not just adding parameters. Architectures combining residual connections, modern activations, and aggressive regularization will achieve superior accuracy-efficiency trade-offs compared to simply scaling existing templates.\"**\n\n### **Immediate Next Steps:**\n\n1.  **Diagnostic Run (1-2 trials):** Take your best-performing architecture (7+ layers, ~75 filters), add residual connections and increase dropout to 0.4. Train with cosine annealing from LR=0.0003.\n2.  **Ablation Matrix (4-6 trials):** Test batch norm's true effect in controlled conditions: two identical architectures (one with BN, one without), matched on all other hyperparameters.\n3.  **Efficiency Metric:** Track **accuracy gain per million parameters** and **accuracy per training hour** alongside raw accuracy. A model with 30% fewer parameters achieving within 0.5% accuracy is a strategic win.\n\n**Strategic Priority:** Move from unconstrained scaling to *intelligent architecture design*. The 3.2\u00d7 parameter increase for 0.34 accuracy gain is unsustainable. The next breakthrough will come from better architectural priors (residuals, modern activations) and tailored regularization, not just more layers.", "experiment_name": "2D_GAF", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_2D_gaf_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "2D Gramian Angular Field representation for SERS bacterial classification"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.05450000000000001, 0.009593211361169815, 0.0033071802826361227, 1.134292944431305, 0.1724664791107178]</data>
</edge>
