<edge source="2D_GAF_bc7d03c0" target="2D_GAF_bc7d03c0" id="memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 150 (for t...)">
  <data key="d3">memorize_finding(finding=Consolidated summary at step 150 (for t...)</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]</data>
  <data key="d6">[[-0.05, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]]</data>
  <data key="d7">{"finding": "Consolidated summary at step 150 (for the last block of research):\n# **STRATEGIC LESSON: Next-Phase Optimization Framework**\n\n## **CORE INSIGHT**\nModel capacity is the dominant accuracy driver, but current scaling leads to inefficient overfitting. The batch normalization paradox suggests either a flawed implementation or a critical interaction effect that invalidates standard design assumptions.\n\n## **ACTIONABLE DIRECTIONS**\n\n### **1. KEEP &amp; DOUBLE-DOWN**\n- **Larger architectures:** Maintain trajectory of increased layers/filters, but with scrutiny.\n- **Lower learning rates:** Preserve ~0.0003 as baseline; best results consistently favor slower, stable learning.\n- **Extended training:** Continue longer epochs with patience, as learning speed strongly correlates with final accuracy.\n\n### **2. CHANGE IMMEDIATELY**\n- **Suspend batch normalization** entirely until root cause of -1.0 correlation is diagnosed. This is likely an experimental artifact.\n- **Shift regularization strategy:** Weak correlations suggest current dropout/weight decay approaches are ineffective. Replace with:\n  - **Architectural regularization:** Introduce skip connections or stochastic depth.\n  - **Sharpness-aware minimization** instead of traditional L2.\n- **Redesign experiment to test interactions:** Next phase must vary batch size \u00d7 learning rate \u00d7 optimizer systematically.\n\n### **3. INVESTIGATE CRITICALLY**\n- **The overfitting paradox:** Best models show 8.7% generalization gap despite larger size. Is this dataset-specific or architectural?\n- **Weight decay's dual nature:** Why does it correlate with instability (0.473) but not accuracy? Likely interacts poorly with other components.\n- **Learning speed metric:** Define and leverage this strong predictor (0.762 correlation) for early stopping.\n\n## **NEW HYPOTHESIS**\n**\"Optimal performance requires balanced capacity scaling with targeted regularization that adapts to architecture depth, not generic dropout/L2.\"**\n\nSpecifically test:\n1. **Efficient scaling hypothesis:** Accuracy gains diminish as parameters increase 3.2\u00d7 for 33% improvement. Is there a sweet spot?\n2. **Interaction hypothesis:** Regularization effectiveness depends on model size (test via partial correlation analysis).\n3. **Topology hypothesis:** Network connectivity (skip/residual) matters more than layer count alone.\n\n## **NEXT-PHASE PRIORITIES**\n1. **Diagnose batch normalization failure** before any further architecture changes.\n2. **Fix train-test gap** in best performers; this is the critical bottleneck.\n3. **Systematically explore activation functions &amp; optimizers** (currently absent variables).\n4. **Report statistics rigorously:** Confidence intervals, effect sizes, multiple comparison corrections.\n\n## **STRATEGIC PRINCIPLE**\nMove from \"more layers \u2192 better accuracy\" to \"balanced architecture \u2192 efficient accuracy.\" The 3.2\u00d7 parameter increase for 33% accuracy gain is unsustainable; next phase must improve parameter efficiency while maintaining stability.\n\n**Immediate experiment:** Contrast two scaled models\u2014one with simple depth increase, one with efficient topology (skip connections, selective regularization)\u2014controlling for total parameters.", "experiment_name": "2D_GAF", "_data_source": "data_paper_mine_csv", "_manifest_name": "SERS_2D_gaf_v1", "_dataset_params": {"n_fft": 512, "hop_length": 64, "gaf_image_size": 48, "video_num_segments": 8, "video_gaf_image_size": 24, "info": "2D Gramian Angular Field representation for SERS bacterial classification"}}</data>
  <data key="d8">[-0.0393881, 0.028523109113913772, 0.003048453440449456, 1.4035586613035202, 0.4331404048919678]</data>
</edge>
